{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"73c6a89ea62e58d4c592368e9a6fbbac4fee81de1ad4680d6327e0da774b5c15","slug":"index-12","location":"/chapters/ch12_video_features/0_index.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"12 - Video Features","authors":[{"id":"Roger Weber","name":"Roger Weber"}],"content_includes_title":false,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"0_index.md","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/0_index-90840282350b25495e2f66100a553686.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"“Tears of Steel”, a short film that the Blender Foundation released under a Creative Commons license, runs for 12 minutes and 14 seconds. At 24 frames per second, that is 17,620 images, together with a soundtrack and a subtitle file. A viewer asks for the scene in which Celia accuses Thom of being afraid of her robot hand. The subtitles answer quickly: the line “Why don’t you just admit that you’re freaked out by my robot hand?” appears twice, at 0:30 in the breakup on a bridge, and at 5:47 when the breakup is replayed forty years later. But a subtitle covers about three seconds of speech, not a scene. Where does the scene start, and where does it end?","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"V1yWSfsOSi"}],"key":"tX1tlNJTrU"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Other requests find nothing in the text at all. The subtitle file holds 76 entries, and between 1:03 and 1:57 it has none. A viewer who wants the fight against the robots and searches the subtitles for “robot” finds six lines. Every one of them belongs to the breakup dialogue, its replay, or the scientists’ announcement that the robot’s memory is synced. None of them is spoken during the fight. What the viewer wants is shown, not said.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"N9GyRMfLTu"}],"key":"xaeH3Anyqx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A video is a sequence of images with sound and text on a common time line, and a search over it returns a stretch of that time line, not the whole file. The 17,620 frames of the film are too many and too similar to index one by one. The basic retrieval unit is therefore the shot, a continuous recording by one camera that ends at a cut or at a gradual transition such as a dissolve. Consecutive shots that share a location, a time, or an action form a scene, such as the breakup on the bridge. The full hierarchy, up to the complete episode, is introduced in ","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"jij7PvuL89"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Time-Based Media and Specialized Solutions","key":"zb6eL0wxXb"}],"identifier":"content-analysis-time-specialized","label":"content-analysis-time-specialized","url":"/time-and-specialized-solutions","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/time-and-specialized-solutions.json","key":"AZgA1qzAbw"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"sU9GlGZgyP"}],"key":"ikSvTbX2Ja"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This chapter finds and describes these units in three steps. First, we split the video into shots: a hard cut shows up as a jump in the distance between the color histograms of consecutive frames, while a dissolve spreads the change over many frames and needs a second threshold. Second, we look inside the shots, because motion carries meaning that no single frame shows. With a static camera, the difference between frames reveals moving objects; with a moving camera, optical flow estimates how every pixel moves. Third, we combine the modalities. Current systems embed keyframes and queries in one vector space, align transcripts and audio events on the time line, and snap each result to the shot boundaries. A football match, where crowd noise and commentary point to the goals, shows the complete pipeline. At every step, a learned model now does the work of a classical method, and we compare the two on the film.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"DjSP88fkGj"}],"key":"NvmwmgCAAH"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"tip","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What you’ll learn","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"M5MXy6q9oQ"}],"key":"z9W0KqmPLh"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain why the shot is the basic retrieval unit of video, and detect hard cuts with histogram distances and a threshold chosen from the ROC curve.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"tFQ0jhnXw7"}],"key":"tpvbMTbLpp"}],"key":"pNy8IGpmix"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Detect gradual transitions with twin thresholds, and select keyframes that represent a shot.","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"W395MPWDak"}],"key":"mfDrq2D0fp"}],"key":"J8CSXiB7Z3"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Detect moving objects with a static camera by background subtraction, frame differencing, and motion history images, and turn the detected motion into a feature for retrieval.","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"mobFwKKAIN"}],"key":"nRanDaf30B"}],"key":"ORmB5SyDQ6"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Derive the Lucas-Kanade optical flow from brightness constancy, and explain the aperture problem and why the method needs corner points.","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"jDoP7fUeL3"}],"key":"S8uAfw08Xw"}],"key":"TTyUoYaxHD"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe how keyframe embeddings, transcripts, and audio events on a common time line find moments in a video, and compare learned models with the classical methods.","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"aul3j2smyZ"}],"key":"rzBMTNdsZn"}],"key":"FO2vFIPcyk"}],"key":"QejsHl5EmK"}],"key":"hmvSs5TAR9"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"seealso","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prerequisites","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"YHwLplZWKp"}],"key":"uex3sBAWIv"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Frames, shots, scenes, and episodes as retrieval units, and the alignment of modalities on the time line: ","position":{"st